{"step_index":20,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-20T17:40:23Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-20T23:10:23+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-20T23:10:24+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/priyanshu%20readme/skills/deep-researcher/references/evidence-by-question-type.md`\nTotal Lines: 97\nTotal Bytes: 4565\nShowing lines 1 to 97\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # What a good source looks like, by question type\n2: \n3: Read only the section that matches the question. In each, sources are ordered\n4: from strongest to weakest, followed by the check that catches the most common\n5: failure for that type. The orderings for health and software rest on measured\n6: findings; those for business, news and creator platforms are standard expert\n7: practice. The rule underneath all of them: get as close as you can to whoever\n8: produced the fact, then check the thing that usually goes wrong in that field.\n9: \n10: ## Health, medicine, science\n11: \n12: 1. Current clinical guideline, systematic review or meta-analysis (Cochrane,\n13:    national health bodies)\n14: 2. Large randomised or pre-registered trial\n15: 3. Observational study\n16: 4. Preprint — usable, labelled as a preprint; many are never published\n17: 5. Press release about a study\n18: 6. News coverage of the press release\n19: \n20: Checks: search the paper title with \"retraction\" (retracted papers keep being\n21: cited for years). Check that the journal is a recognised one; \"peer-reviewed\"\n22: on the journal's own site proves nothing. Read the abstract or full text, not\n23: the news summary. One new study does not overturn a review of many. Note\n24: sample size, who was studied, and who funded it.\n25: \n26: ## Statistics and \"X% of...\" claims\n27: \n28: 1. The producing body's own dataset or release, with its methodology note\n29: 2. A report that reproduces the table and links to it\n30: 3. A news article quoting it\n31: 4. Infographics, listicles, social posts\n32: \n33: Checks: what was measured, out of what, on what sample, when; headline figure\n34: or subgroup; latest release or an old one. \"50% more than\" and \"50% of\" are\n35: different claims and get swapped constantly. If the origin cannot be found,\n36: the number is a lead, not a finding, and he should not quote it.\n37: \n38: ## Software, tools, AI models, APIs\n39: \n40: 1. Source code, release notes, changelog, official docs for the version in use\n41: 2. Issue tracker and maintainer comments\n42: 3. Standards and specifications\n43: 4. Engineering blogs from teams that run the thing in production\n44: 5. Q&A sites — check the answer's date and version\n45: 6. Tutorials and content-marketing blogs\n46: \n47: Checks: version and date. Being out of date, not dishonesty, is the main\n48: failure here; many answers are obsolete when posted. For anything released in\n49: the last few weeks, official sources only, since everything else predates it.\n50: Benchmarks published by a vendor about its own product are claims until an\n51: independent party reproduces them.\n52: \n53: ## Market size, business, money\n54: \n55: 1. Audited filings (annual reports, 10-K, prospectus), regulators, national\n56:    statistics\n57: 2. Earnings calls and investor decks — primary for what the company claims,\n58:    not for whether it is true\n59: 3. Named analyst reports with a disclosed method\n60: 4. Press-release summaries of paid market reports — leads only\n61: 5. Blogs quoting those\n62: \n63: Checks: treat any \"USD X billion by 20YY at Z% CAGR\" sentence as marketing\n64: until you can see the method. Different firms define the same market\n65: differently, so report a range with the definitions, never a single figure.\n66: Income claims from people selling a course are advertisements.\n67: \n68: ## News and current events\n69: \n70: 1. The outlet that did the original reporting; wire services (AP, Reuters,\n71:    AFP); primary documents (court filings, official statements, transcripts)\n72: 2. Outlets that credit and link the above\n73: 3. Aggregators and rewrites\n74: 4. Social posts\n75: \n76: Checks: follow \"first reported by\" credits and publication times to find the\n77: originating outlet. For breaking stories, early reports are often wrong; date\n78: everything and prefer later corrected accounts.\n79: \n80: ## YouTube, creator platforms, algorithm and monetisation claims\n81: \n82: 1. The platform's own documentation, policy pages, official blog and official\n83:    staff channels (for YouTube: Help Center, Creator Insider, official\n84:    statements from named staff)\n85: 2. Studies over many channels or videos with a stated sample and method\n86: 3. A named creator's own analytics, shown on screen, for their own channel —\n87:    evidence about that channel only\n88: 4. \"What the algorithm wants\" videos and blogs, tool vendors' growth claims,\n89:    anonymous forum advice — leads only\n90: \n91: Checks: policy and monetisation rules change often and differ by country, so\n92: confirm on the current policy page and note the date you read it. A tactic\n93: that worked for one channel is an anecdote; look for whether it holds across\n94: many, and whether the person recommending it sells something that depends on\n95: it. RPM, CPM and revenue figures vary widely by niche, audience country and\n96: season, so report ranges with their source, not single numbers.\n97: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
